FAQ = [
 ("What are the best running boards for a 2015–2020 Ford F-150?",
  "Rough Country's RPT2 is the best running board for a 2015–2020 F-150 SuperCrew. It is a steel board with a 6.75 in step, the widest on this page, a no-drill install and a limited lifetime warranty. Rough Country's HD2 is the one-piece aluminum board for salt states. SuperCab owners should buy Westin's PRO TRAXX 5 21-53935, which Westin lists for the SuperCab through 2026, and a SuperCrew with the 6.5 ft bed can use Westin's 21-534635 wheel-to-wheel bar. AMP's PowerStep Smart Series is the power step for anyone who needs the lowest step. Match the cab first; the Rough Country picks are Crew Cab only."),
 ("Which running boards fit a 2015–2020 F-150 SuperCrew?",
  "Any board or bar sold for the 2015–2020 F-150 Crew Cab or SuperCrew. On this page that is the Rough Country RPT2, HD2 and BA2 (Rough Country says all three fit Crew Cab models only), the Westin 21-534635 wheel-to-wheel bar (listed for the 2015–2020 SuperCrew with the 6.5 ft bed) and AMP Research's PowerStep Smart Series 86151-01A, which retailers list for the 2015–2020 F-150 without naming a cab. Westin's cab-length PRO TRAXX 5 for the SuperCrew is 21-53945 in black. Read the title for the cab before you order, because a SuperCab bar is shorter and its brackets land in different places."),
 ("Do 2015–2020 F-150 running boards fit a 2021+ F-150?",
  "Often, for the same cab. Westin's own page lists the PRO TRAXX 5 part numbers 21-53935 (SuperCab) and 21-53945 (SuperCrew) for 2015–2026, and Rough Country lists the RPT2, HD2 and BA2 for 2015–2026 Crew Cab. The exception on this page is the power step: AMP's Smart Series 86151-01A is listed for 2015–2020, and retailers show a separate part for the 2021+ truck. If a listing names your year, it fits; if it stops at 2020, do not assume the newer truck. Our 2021–2026 F-150 running boards guide covers the 14th gen."),
 ("Did the 2015–2020 F-150 come with factory running boards?",
  "Some did. Wikipedia's page on this generation says the Limited trim came standard with power running boards, along with 22 in wheels and the 3.5L EcoBoost. We could not confirm from Ford material which other trims had fixed boards as standard or optional, so look at your truck. If it has boards, the aftermarket set replaces them: you remove the factory boards and brackets first. If it has power boards, ask the seller whether the aftermarket kit is meant to replace them, and whether any factory wiring stays in place."),
 ("Are power running boards worth it on an F-150?",
  "They are if someone who rides in the truck has trouble with the step height, or if you want the boards out of sight for a clean look and clearance. AMP's PowerStep Smart Series drops a step when a door opens and folds it up when the door closes, with LED lights and an app to hold the steps up or down. CARiD lists it at $1,949.99, which is about four times a Rough Country RPT2. Rough Country lists its own power running boards for the 2015–2026 F-150 from $1,299.95 and an HT-Series from $999.95, and both are in our product list for comparison."),
 ("What is a wheel-to-wheel nerf bar and do I need one?",
  "A cab-length bar ends at the rear door. A wheel-to-wheel bar runs on to the rear wheel well, so there is a step beside the bed. On a 13th-gen F-150 with the 6.5 ft box, that extra step helps when you reach over the side for a toolbox or tie-down. Westin's 21-534635 is the wheel-to-wheel PRO TRAXX 5 listed for the 2015–2020 SuperCrew with the 6.5 ft bed. It is bed-length specific, so a 5.5 ft SuperCrew needs a different part. Confirm your bed on Westin's fit tool before ordering."),
 ("Do I need to drill to install running boards on a 2015–2020 F-150?",
  "Not with the parts on this page. Rough Country describes the RPT2, HD2 and BA2 as no-drill, bolt-on installs, and Westin says the PRO TRAXX 5 is bolt-on with no drilling required on most applications and includes a mount kit. The brackets use the factory mounting points on the rocker and body. The one kit that takes real work is the AMP PowerStep, which needs power, a ground and the OBD connector plugged in; CARiD lists it with an OBD connector and a wiring harness."),
 ("Steel or aluminum boards for a truck in a salt state?",
  "Both can work; the coating decides. Rough Country's HD2 is one-piece aluminum, which Rough Country calls corrosion-resistant, and aluminum does not rust the way bare steel does. The RPT2 and BA2 are steel with a black finish, and Rough Country covers the finish for one year on the RPT2 and five years on the BA2. Westin's polished 304 stainless PRO TRAXX 5 carries a lifetime warranty on the finish, while the black powder coat version is covered for three years. If roads are salted where you live, rinse the brackets and the underside of the boards through winter."),
 ("How much weight can these running boards hold?",
  "Only one maker on this page publishes a figure. CARiD lists a 600 lb load capacity for the AMP PowerStep Smart Series. Rough Country's RPT2, HD2 and BA2 pages do not state a load rating, and Westin's PRO TRAXX 5 page does not either. For a step that carries an adult climbing in, every board here is built for that use, but if you plan to stand on a board to load a roof rack, ask the seller for the rating before you buy."),
 ("Do these fit the 2017–2020 F-150 Raptor?",
  "Rough Country lists the RPT2, HD2 and BA2 for the 2017–2026 F-150 Raptor Crew Cab, so a SuperCrew Raptor is covered by those three. The Raptor also came as a SuperCab, and we did not see a SuperCab Raptor named in any listing here, so a SuperCab Raptor owner should ask the seller. Keep in mind the Raptor's wider body and higher ride; a drop-style step or a wide board hangs lower on a truck that goes off-road, where rock sliders are the better choice."),
 ("What about the Regular Cab?",
  "We did not find a Regular Cab part on Amazon with a title that names this truck, so there is no Regular Cab pick on this page. Westin's PRO TRAXX 5 page shows only SuperCab and SuperCrew numbers for the 2015–2026 F-150, and Rough Country's three boards are Crew Cab only. Use Westin's or Rough Country's fit tool with your cab selected, or ask the seller for a part number that names the Regular Cab. A SuperCab bar is too long for the two-door body."),
]

