  {"h": "Cab length, and the SuperCab trap",
   "body": "The 13th-gen F-150 came in three cabs: a two-door Regular Cab, the SuperCab with rear-hinged half doors, and the four-door SuperCrew. A running board or nerf bar is cut to the cab, and its brackets land on mounting points that move with the cab length, so a SuperCrew board does not fit a SuperCab. Westin's PRO TRAXX 5 page makes the split plain: 21-53935 is the black SuperCab bar and 21-53945 the black SuperCrew bar, both listed for 2015–2026. Rough Country's RPT2, HD2 and BA2 pages all say Crew Cab only. The SuperCab was common on XL and XLT work trucks and on the 2017–2020 Raptor, and it has fewer choices, which is why one pick here is a SuperCab-specific bar. Regular Cab owners have the fewest options and should use a maker's fit tool."},
  {"h": "Nerf bar, flat board, drop step or power step",
   "body": "The step style decides who the truck is easy for. Westin's PRO TRAXX 5 is an oval tube with molded step pads, a narrow step with a clean look that sits close to the body. Rough Country's RPT2 is a flat steel board with what Rough Country calls a 6.75 in stepping area, and the BA2 a 5 in one; a flat board gives kids and older passengers a platform rather than a tube. Drop steps hang a lower step at each door for a lifted truck, and APS pocket steps in our product list are a budget version of that idea. A power step, such as AMP's PowerStep Smart Series, folds under the rocker when the doors are closed and drops when one opens. It gives the lowest step and the most clearance, at the highest price."},
  {"h": "Which parts carry over to the 2021+ truck",
   "body": "The makers' fit lists show that most cab-mounted steps carry over to the 2021 redesign. Westin lists 21-53935 and 21-53945 for 2015–2026, and Rough Country lists the RPT2, HD2 and BA2 for 2015–2026 Crew Cab, including the Lightning and Tremor that arrived later. That means a board bought for a 2016 can move to a newer F-150. The power step is different: AMP's Smart Series 86151-01A is listed by Motomentum for the 2015–2020 F-150 only, and retailers show a separate 2021+ part. So if you are shopping used or planning to trade up, check the title's year range. A title that stops at 2020 is a fit for this truck and nothing newer."},
  {"h": "Material, finish and the warranty behind it",
   "body": "The warranty terms are the clearest signal of how a maker expects a step to age. Rough Country covers the RPT2 and HD2 with a limited lifetime warranty against material and workmanship failures for the original purchaser, with the finish covered for one year; the BA2 carries a 5-year limited warranty. Westin covers polished 304 stainless PRO TRAXX 5 bars for life against defects in materials, workmanship and finish, and the black powder coat over e-coated steel for three years. AMP backs the PowerStep Smart Series for 5 years or 60,000 miles. On a truck in a salt state, the HD2's aluminum or Westin's stainless avoid rust at chips, while a black steel board needs its finish to hold."},
  {"h": "Install, wiring and clearance",
   "body": "Every pick here bolts to factory mounting points without drilling, by the makers' descriptions: Rough Country calls all three boards no-drill, bolt-on kits, and Westin says the PRO TRAXX 5 is bolt-on with no drilling on most applications and ships with a mount kit. Plan on a helper to hold the board while you start the bolts, and recheck torque after a week. The AMP PowerStep adds wiring: CARiD lists it with an OBD connector and a wiring harness, and the Smart Series app controls the steps and lights. On clearance, a fixed board or bar is the lowest point on the side of the truck; a power step tucks up out of the way. For a Raptor or any truck that sees rocks, rock sliders protect the rocker better than a step."},
